Most of the codebase originates from AutoDock, while the CUDA-specific files implement or support the GPU acceleration experiments.
Why CUDA?
Molecular docking explores many possible molecular configurations and repeatedly evaluates their associated energies.
Many of these calculations can potentially be performed independently. GPUs are designed to execute large numbers of parallel operations, making CUDA a natural platform for investigating acceleration of these workloads.
Conceptually:
CPU

configuration 1 ---> energy
configuration 2 ---> energy
configuration 3 ---> energy
configuration 4 ---> energy


GPU

configuration 1 --+
configuration 2 --+
configuration 3 --+--> parallel energy evaluation
configuration 4 --+
       ...        |
configuration N --+
The objective is to exploit this parallelism while preserving the docking behavior of the underlying AutoDock implementation.
Use Cases
This repository may be useful for:
- GPU computing experiments
- CUDA optimization studies
- Molecular docking research
- Computational chemistry experiments
- High-performance computing studies
- Parallel algorithm research
- Learning how legacy scientific software can be adapted for GPU execution
Project Status
This repository should be considered experimental/research software.
It is particularly useful as a reference for investigating CUDA integration with an existing scientific C/C++ codebase.
For production molecular docking workloads, consider actively maintained AutoDock projects and validate results carefully before using them for scientific conclusions.
Background
AutoDock is a suite of automated molecular docking tools designed to predict how small molecules bind to receptors with known three-dimensional structures.
Typical applications include:
- Structure-based drug design
- Virtual screening
- Lead optimization
- Protein-ligand docking
- Chemical mechanism studies
This repository explores how parts of that computational workload can be accelerated using GPU parallelism.
